/* Philosophy / principle of the day: title, thinker or source, idea, ELI8, apply */
const IDEAS=[
{t:"Consistency beats intensity",w:"The Prophet ﷺ; Bayan daily practice",i:"The most beloved deeds to Allah are the most consistent, even if small (Bukhari 6464). A plan you repeat beats a plan you admire.",k:"Doing a little every day is better than doing a lot once and stopping.",d:"Keep today's habits small enough to repeat tomorrow."},
{t:"The golden mean",w:"Aristotle, Nicomachean Ethics; echoed by Ibn Miskawayh and al-Ghazali",i:"Virtue lies between two vices: courage between cowardice and recklessness, generosity between stinginess and waste. Moderation is not weakness but calibration.",k:"The best way is usually in the middle: not too scared, not too wild; not too stingy, not too wasteful.",d:"Find the middle in one thing today: food, spending, or screen time."},
{t:"Attention shapes reality",w:"Bayan daily practice; William James",i:"What you attend to grows. A day spent attending to complaints becomes a complaining life; a day attending to blessings becomes a grateful one.",k:"What you look at a lot becomes big in your mind. Look at good things.",d:"Choose what you will pay attention to for the next hour, on purpose."},
{t:"Knowledge without action is wasted",w:"Imam 'Ali, Nahj al-Balagha (received); Bayan daily practice",i:"Knowledge calls out to action; if it is answered, it stays, otherwise it departs. Learning is completed by doing.",k:"Knowing something is only half. Doing it is the other half.",d:"Take one thing you learned this week and do it today."},
{t:"The dichotomy of control",w:"Epictetus, Enchiridion; parallels in tawakkul",i:"Some things are up to us (our judgements, efforts, choices) and some are not (outcomes, other people, the weather). Peace comes from working on the first and accepting the second.",k:"Some things you can control, like trying hard. Some things you cannot, like the weather. Worry only about the first kind.",d:"Write two columns: what I control today, what I do not. Act on the first."},
{t:"'Asabiyyah: the strength of the group",w:"Ibn Khaldun, Muqaddimah (1377)",i:"Ibn Khaldun argued that civilisations rise on group solidarity and fall when luxury dissolves it. A family is the smallest civilisation; its 'asabiyyah is built at the dinner table.",k:"Groups that stick together are strong. A family that eats together and helps each other is strong.",d:"Do one thing tonight that only works if the whole family joins."},
{t:"Habit is second nature",w:"Al-Ghazali, Ihya 'Ulum al-Din; Aristotle",i:"Character is not chosen in a moment; it is the sediment of repeated acts. Al-Ghazali's cure for a bad trait is to practise its opposite until it becomes natural.",k:"If you do something many times, it becomes part of you. So practise good things.",d:"Pick one trait you want and do its smallest action three times today."},
{t:"Bani Adam: one body",w:"Saadi Shirazi, Gulistan (1258)",i:"Human beings are members of one body, created from one essence. When one member suffers, the others cannot stay at rest. Inscribed at the United Nations.",k:"All people are like parts of one body. If someone hurts, we should feel it too.",d:"Give to one person outside your family today."},
{t:"Khudi: the self made strong",w:"Muhammad Iqbal, Asrar-i-Khudi (1915)",i:"Iqbal taught that the self is strengthened by love, striving and faith until destiny itself asks what you want. Self-respect, not self-indulgence.",k:"Make yourself so strong and good that Allah asks you what you wish for.",d:"Do the hard thing first this morning."},
{t:"The examined life",w:"Socrates, in Plato's Apology",i:"The unexamined life is not worth living. A few minutes of honest reflection each day is the difference between living and merely passing time.",k:"Think about your day before you sleep: what went well, what did not. That is how you grow.",d:"Fill in the reflection journal tonight."},
{t:"Opportunity cost",w:"Economics; Frédéric Bastiat's 'what is not seen'",i:"Every choice is also a choice against something else. An hour of scrolling costs an hour of reading or walking. The cost is real even when invisible.",k:"When you choose one thing, you are also choosing not to do another thing. Choose carefully.",d:"Before one leisure choice today, name what it costs."},
{t:"Marginal gains",w:"Sports science; kaizen",i:"Improve many small things by one percent and the sum is large. OnePlate is built on this: a thumb of oil, a walk, a roti, a bottle of water.",k:"Lots of tiny improvements add up to a big one.",d:"Make one thing one percent better today."},
{t:"Muraqabah: self-watching",w:"Al-Muhasibi; the Sufi tradition",i:"Muraqabah is the practice of knowing you are seen. Muhasabah is taking account of yourself before you are taken to account. Together they are the engine of character.",k:"Remember Allah sees you, and check your own actions like a kind teacher.",d:"Pause three times today and ask: what am I doing, and why?"},
{t:"Division of responsibility",w:"Ellyn Satter; adopted by Thuluth",i:"Parents decide what, when and where food is served; children decide whether and how much. Pressure at the table backfires.",k:"Mama and Baba choose the food; you choose how much you eat. Nobody forces anybody.",d:"Tonight, serve the plate and say nothing about how much is eaten."},
{t:"First principles",w:"Aristotle; Ibn Sina's method",i:"Break a problem to its basic truths and reason up from there, instead of reasoning by analogy. 'Why do we eat this way?' answered from physiology and revelation, not from fashion.",k:"Ask 'why' until you reach the real reason, then build from there.",d:"Take one rule you follow and trace it back to its reason."},
{t:"The virtuous city",w:"Al-Farabi, Al-Madina al-Fadila",i:"Al-Farabi held that a good society is one where each member fulfils a role toward shared happiness, like organs in a body. The household is the first such city.",k:"A good family is like a good team: everyone has a job that helps the others.",d:"Give each family member one small role at dinner tonight."},
{t:"Grief as a guest",w:"Rumi, Masnavi; 'The Guest House'",i:"Rumi treats every emotion as a visitor: welcome it, learn from it, let it leave. Emotions acknowledged pass through; emotions resisted move in.",k:"Feelings are like visitors. Say hello to sad feelings too, and they will leave sooner.",d:"Name today's strongest feeling in one word on the mood check-in."},
{t:"Ask, do not assume",w:"The Quran's command to consult (3:159); Socratic method",i:"Consultation is a method for discovering what you do not know you do not know. Leaders who ask are corrected before they fail.",k:"Ask other people what they think before deciding. They might see something you missed.",d:"Ask your spouse one question whose answer you think you already know."},
{t:"Know thyself",w:"The Delphic maxim; 'man 'arafa nafsahu' in the Islamic tradition",i:"Self-knowledge is the beginning of all knowledge. A saying widely circulated in Islamic spirituality holds that whoever knows himself knows his Lord; its chain is not established, but its use is ancient.",k:"Knowing yourself, what you like, what makes you angry, what you are good at, helps you know everything else.",d:"Write one true sentence about yourself you have never said out loud."},
{t:"The pen is mightier",w:"Quran 96:4 and 68:1; the Bayan Library's founding verse 55:4",i:"Revelation began with reading and swore by the pen. Civilisation is what is written down and read again. A family that reads together compounds its knowledge across generations.",k:"Writing and reading are how smart ideas travel from one person to another, even hundreds of years later.",d:"Read aloud to the children for ten minutes tonight."},
{t:"Sleep is a sign",w:"Quran 30:23; sleep science",i:"Your sleep by night and your seeking of His bounty are among His signs. Seven to eight hours is not laziness; it is when the body repairs, the mind files, and appetite hormones reset.",k:"Sleep is when your body fixes itself and your brain tidies up. It is important, not lazy.",d:"Lights out by 10:45 tonight."},
{t:"Enough is a decision",w:"Qana'ah in the Islamic tradition; Epicurus on simple pleasures",i:"Sufficiency is not a quantity but a judgement. The stop point at 80 percent full is a decision made before the meal, not a feeling found during it.",k:"'Enough' is something you decide, not something your tummy tells you late.",d:"Decide your portion before you sit down."},
{t:"Serve to lead",w:"The Prophet ﷺ: the leader of a people is their servant (received); Imam 'Ali's letter to Malik",i:"Authority in the Islamic tradition is framed as service. The father who cooks, carries and consults leads more than the one who commands.",k:"A good leader helps the people he leads. Baba helping at home is leading.",d:"Do one chore today that is usually someone else's."},
{t:"Memento mori, tadhkirat al-mawt",w:"Stoics; the Prophet ﷺ: remember often the destroyer of pleasures (Tirmidhi 2307)",i:"Remembering death is not morbid; it is a lens that sorts the important from the urgent. Today's dinner together is important. Most notifications are not.",k:"Remembering that life does not last forever helps you spend time on what matters, like family.",d:"Do the one thing you would regret not doing."},
{t:"Beauty is a duty",w:"The Prophet ﷺ: Allah is beautiful and loves beauty (Muslim 91); ihsan",i:"Doing a thing beautifully, a plate arranged with care, a room kept tidy, a voice kept soft, is itself an act of worship in the tradition.",k:"Making things beautiful, like setting the table nicely, is a way of thanking Allah.",d:"Make tonight's plate look as good as it tastes."}
];
/* Book of the day: title, author, year, why, core idea, takeaway, next */
const BOOKS=[
{t:"The Muqaddimah",w:"Ibn Khaldun",y:"1377",why:"The founding text of sociology and the philosophy of history, written in a fortress in Algeria.",core:"Civilisations rise on group solidarity and decline when luxury erodes it; history has laws that can be studied.",take:"Strength is social before it is material.",next:"Read the chapters on 'asabiyyah and on the sciences."},
{t:"The Canon of Medicine",w:"Ibn Sina (Avicenna)",y:"1025",core:"A complete system of medicine in five books, from theory to drugs, used in European universities for six hundred years.",why:"It made medicine systematic and gave diet and exercise a place in prevention.",take:"Ibn Sina put food, sleep, movement and emotion among the six necessities of health. OnePlate agrees.",next:"Read the first book's section on the regimen of health."},
{t:"The Masnavi",w:"Jalal al-Din Rumi",y:"1258–1273",why:"Six books of stories and teaching in Persian couplets, called the Quran in the Persian tongue.",core:"Every story is a door to the inner life; the reed flute's lament is the soul's longing for its source.",take:"Read a story, then ask what it says about you.",next:"Start with the story of the reed and the parable of the elephant in the dark."},
{t:"The Gulistan",w:"Saadi Shirazi",y:"1258",why:"The most read book of Persian prose for seven centuries; taught to children across the Persianate world.",core:"Moral anecdotes in rhymed prose: on kings, dervishes, contentment, silence, youth, old age, manners.",take:"Bani Adam: humanity is one body.",next:"Read chapter one, 'On the conduct of kings', at the dinner table, one story a night."},
{t:"The Revival of the Religious Sciences",w:"Abu Hamid al-Ghazali",y:"c. 1100",why:"The most influential work of Islamic spirituality, reconnecting law to the inner life.",core:"Forty books covering worship, daily life, the vices that destroy and the virtues that save.",take:"The book on the discipline of the soul describes habit formation eight centuries before psychology did.",next:"Read 'On Disciplining the Soul' and 'On Breaking the Two Desires' (food and sex): the Islamic source of moderation in eating."},
{t:"Nicomachean Ethics",w:"Aristotle",y:"c. 340 BCE",why:"The first systematic account of virtue, happiness and the good life, and a direct influence on Islamic ethics.",core:"Happiness is activity in accordance with virtue; virtue is a habit lying in a mean.",take:"You become brave by doing brave things.",next:"Read Book II on habit and the mean."},
{t:"The Republic",w:"Plato",y:"c. 375 BCE",why:"The founding text of political philosophy, read by al-Farabi and Ibn Rushd.",core:"Justice in the city mirrors justice in the soul: reason ruling spirit and appetite.",take:"A just person is one whose appetite does not rule.",next:"Read the allegory of the cave in Book VII."},
{t:"The Analects",w:"Confucius",y:"c. 475–221 BCE",why:"The foundation of two thousand years of East Asian ethics and education.",core:"Benevolence, ritual, filial piety and self-cultivation; the gentleman is made, not born.",take:"'Is it not a joy to learn and practise what you have learned?' is its opening line.",next:"Read Book I."},
{t:"Meditations",w:"Marcus Aurelius",y:"c. 170",why:"A Roman emperor's private notebook of Stoic practice.",core:"Control what you can, accept what you cannot, do your duty today.",take:"Begin each morning expecting difficult people, and be unsurprised.",next:"Read Book II, entry one."},
{t:"Confessions",w:"Augustine of Hippo",y:"c. 400",why:"The first autobiography of the inner life, including a famous chapter on the pull of appetite.",core:"The restless heart; memory; time; the long turning toward God.",take:"'Our heart is restless until it rests in You.'",next:"Read Book X on memory."},
{t:"Don Quixote",w:"Miguel de Cervantes",y:"1605–1615",why:"The first modern novel, and still the funniest.",core:"A man who reads too many romances sets out to be a knight; the world and his squire answer back.",take:"Idealism and realism need each other; Quixote and Sancho are one person.",next:"Read the windmills chapter with the children."},
{t:"The Divan of Hafez",w:"Hafez",y:"c. 1368",why:"The most beloved book of Persian lyric poetry, opened for guidance in homes across Iran and South Asia.",core:"Love, wine as a symbol of ecstasy, the hypocrisy of the pious, the beloved's face.",take:"Hold your heart lightly and your tongue carefully.",next:"Read ten ghazals in Dick Davis's translation."},
{t:"The Shahnameh",w:"Ferdowsi",y:"1010",why:"The Persian national epic: fifty thousand couplets of kings and heroes, written to save a language.",core:"Rostam and Sohrab, Zahhak and Kaveh, the tragedy of fathers and sons.",take:"Jealousy and haste destroy what love built.",next:"Tell the children the story of Zal and the Simurgh."},
{t:"Kulliyat-e-Iqbal",w:"Muhammad Iqbal",y:"1915–1938",why:"The poet-philosopher of Pakistan, read in Urdu and Persian across the subcontinent.",core:"Khudi, the strengthened self; the Shikwa and its answer; the eagle, not the vulture.",take:"'Khudi ko kar buland itna…': raise the self until destiny consults you.",next:"Read 'Bal-e-Jibril' slowly, one poem a week."},
{t:"Al-Sahifa al-Sajjadiyya",w:"Imam 'Ali ibn al-Husayn (attributed)",y:"c. 700",why:"Fifty-four supplications called the Psalms of the Household of the Prophet, with their own chain in the preface.",core:"Prayers for parents, children, neighbours, the sick, the ending of a day, noble character.",take:"The prayer for one's children (Supplication 25) is a parenting manual in the form of a dua.",next:"Read Supplication 20, Makarim al-Akhlaq, and 25, for one's children."},
{t:"Nahj al-Balagha",w:"Sermons, letters and sayings of Imam 'Ali, compiled by al-Sharif al-Radi",y:"c. 1010",why:"The most studied collection of Imam 'Ali's words; the letter to Malik al-Ashtar is read as a charter of governance.",core:"Sermons on creation and piety, letters of administration, and 480 short sayings.",take:"'The worth of a person is what he does well.'",next:"Read Letter 31, to his son, and Letter 53, to Malik."},
{t:"The Prophet of Islam",w:"Sirah literature, from Ibn Ishaq and Ibn Hisham",y:"c. 770",why:"The earliest connected biography of the Prophet, the source for most later sirah.",core:"Makkah, Madinah, the treaties, the conquests, the character.",take:"The life is the commentary on the Book.",next:"Read Martin Lings's 'Muhammad: His Life Based on the Earliest Sources' as a modern retelling."},
{t:"Elements",w:"Euclid",y:"c. 300 BCE",why:"The most successful textbook in history, in use for two thousand years; translated into Arabic in Baghdad.",core:"From five postulates, all of geometry, by proof alone.",take:"Certainty can be built from a few true things.",next:"Prove with the children that the angles of a triangle add to a straight line."},
{t:"The Compendious Book on Calculation",w:"Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi",y:"c. 820",why:"The book that gave the world 'algebra' (al-jabr) and whose author's name became 'algorithm'.",core:"Solving linear and quadratic problems by balancing and completion, for inheritance, trade and surveying.",take:"Mathematics was developed to settle inheritances fairly.",next:"Tell the children where the word algorithm comes from."},
{t:"On the Origin of Species",w:"Charles Darwin",y:"1859",why:"The book that organised biology around a single mechanism.",core:"Variation, inheritance, and selection over long time produce the diversity of life.",take:"Small differences, accumulated, become large ones. True of species and of habits.",next:"Read the final paragraph, 'There is grandeur in this view of life'."},
{t:"The Brothers Karamazov",w:"Fyodor Dostoevsky",y:"1880",why:"The novel of faith and doubt, fathers and sons, free will and suffering.",core:"Three brothers, a murdered father, and the Grand Inquisitor's challenge to freedom.",take:"'Everyone is responsible for everyone.'",next:"Read the chapter 'The Grand Inquisitor' and then Father Zosima's answer."},
{t:"The Thousand and One Nights",w:"Anonymous storytellers",y:"c. 800–1400",why:"The frame story of Shahrazad saving her life, and her sisters', by telling stories.",core:"Sindbad, Ali Baba, Aladdin, and hundreds more, from Baghdad, Cairo and beyond.",take:"Stories can hold back the sword.",next:"Tell the children one Night each week, chosen for its moral."},
{t:"Tao Te Ching",w:"Laozi (traditional attribution)",y:"c. 400 BCE",why:"Eighty-one short chapters on the Way: yielding, simplicity, non-striving.",core:"The soft overcomes the hard; the sage leads by stepping back.",take:"'A journey of a thousand miles begins beneath one's feet.'",next:"Read chapters 8 and 64."},
{t:"Essays",w:"Michel de Montaigne",y:"1580",why:"The invention of the essay as a form of honest self-examination.",core:"On friendship, on cannibals, on experience; what do I know?",take:"Writing about yourself honestly is a way of learning about everyone.",next:"Read 'Of Experience', his last essay, on eating and living well."}
];
